steps.body:
When PostgreSQL picks the ids, which ones did your rows get? Add `RETURNING` to the end of an `INSERT` and it prints the new rows as a result table, just like a `SELECT`.

```sql harness
CREATE TABLE tasks (
    id integer GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title text,
    done boolean DEFAULT false
);
INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES ('Write the outline');
{{code}}
```

```sql
INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES ('Ship it') RETURNING id, title;
```

The list after `RETURNING` works like a `SELECT` list: name columns, use `*` for all of them, or add expressions with `AS`. It shows the values actually stored, so generated ids and defaults come back filled in.

```sql harness
CREATE TABLE tasks (
    id integer GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title text,
    done boolean DEFAULT false
);
INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES ('Write the outline'), ('Ship it');
{{code}}
```

```sql
INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES ('Celebrate') RETURNING *;
```

Use `RETURNING` instead of looking up `max(id)` afterwards. An identity hands out each number once and never takes it back, so an insert that fails still uses one up and leaves a gap. The next id is not always the one you expect, and `RETURNING` tells you the real one.

example.code:
CREATE TABLE tasks (
    id integer GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title text,
    done boolean DEFAULT false,
    priority integer DEFAULT 3
);

INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES ('Ship it'), ('Celebrate') RETURNING id, title;

SELECT * FROM tasks ORDER BY id;

